The hooded stranger looked around the tavern approvingly.  Two dozen
rough and tough (but not too bright) orcs, all eager to hear more
about his offer, had assembled.  Copies of the handbill lay strewn
about the tavern floor and still more were gripped in chubby callused
hands.  Though most had given up trying to decipher the lettering, the
cartoon image on the advertisement conveyed the message clearly
enough: a fat and very happy orc relaxed at a grand dining table with
weapons of war prominently displayed on the wall above him.

Once he had their attention, the hooded stranger made his offer.  A
certain "bauble" had been stolen from him by a particularly nasty
wizard.  He wanted the "bauble" returned to him and in exchange for
this service, the industrious rogue could have whatever treasures he
could recover from the dungeon in addition to the princely sum of One
Million Zorkmids.

Compared to this offer, the dangers of the dungeon were of little
concern to this bunch.  As such, there weren't too many questions from
the crowd as most were eager to be the first into the dungeon.  The
hooded stranger was not willing to offer a retainer as _certainly_ the
winner would not want to see portions of his or her winnings diluted
by outfitting the failures.  (Grunts of approval) But he could offer
this: he had dropped his P.Y. Express Card somewhere in the dungeon on
his last trip and hadn't been able to collect it.  Whoever found it
could use it freely, and certainly a rogue industrious enough to
recover the "bauble" would be able to handle a little identity fraud.
"Check the shops and Delphi, I might have dropped it there."

One rogue in particular happened to be both smarter and more
suspicious than the rest (albeit not by much) and it was this
particular orcish rogue who decided to do some asking around about
this "bauble".  Perhaps to the right entity, this "bauble" could be
worth _more_ than 1,000,000 Zm ...

[5 days later]

Maud the Warrior was enjoying a drink at the Demigod Bar when Eve the
Curator stormed through the doors and slammed the Daily down on the
counter.
"You won't believe this, they'll let anyone in here nowadays.  In my
time, we had to _work_ to get here, now it's every riff-raff that can
pick up a sharp stick has free rein of the place!  Check out this one,
he can't even read!"
"Oh my, look at this fashion outcast, what is he wearing?!"
Eve followed Maud's gaze to the entrance of the bar as Nethack's
newest Demigod strolled into the bar.  Dust rose off of his thoroughly
burnt orcish cloak and blood still dripped from his greasy orcish
chain mail.  The rusty and corroded orcish helm he was wearing looked
to be staying together only by magic.
"Well, there goes the neighborhood!"

