After having figured out a standard Ascension routine that worked for
me, I decided to try something different and add some conducts to my
game.  I thought it would be fun to try an illiterate character and I
thought it would be fitting that he was an orc.  And I thought it
would be an interesting challenge to have a racial/racist character
rather than just end up with the standard Ascension Kit (TM).  But, I
didn't think that I'd stand a very good chance of Ascending without
Magic Resistance so I figured that I would need to wish for a cross-
class quest artifact.  My ideal case, given that the character was a
rogue, was a stolen credit card, but I would have settled for the Orb
of Detection if I didn't find a Neutral altar.  If I didn't find any
cross-aligned altars I would have either wished for Magicbane or else
donned a helm of opposite alignment and converted an altar to
lawful.

It took me a while and I had many humbling learning experiences but I
finally succeeded in ascending a racial and illiterate orcish rogue.
It took 19 tries.  On attempt 16 I choked on a giant spider corpse
while prepping to zap a wand of digging at Rodney's tower.   So,
looking back at my success rate, I'd say that it took me 16 tries to
get to Gehennom, and then I made it to Gehennom on the third try
following that, so I'd like to think that I'm improving.  The hardest
part was staying alive in the early game with just an orcish helm
ususally acquired from an early goblin.  I usually didn't find orcish
chainmail until much later.  Staying alive past the "giant bat" phase
was toughest, followed by the "soldier ant" phase.  Once I managed to
acquire divine protection I could generally level up to XL=14 and the
hard part was over.  I won't soon forget however, that 3 zaps from a
wand of lightning and 100 hp are not necessarily sufficient for
engaging an owlbear in melee.  Did you know that an electric shock
from a chest can instakill a level 1 orcish rogue (11 hp), ouch!

I herded my pet down to Sokoban where I found a bag of holding.  I was
hoping for an amulet of reflection but I think having less early game
weight issues was a big help.  Then still at less than XL=5 I brought
my pets to Minetown where I found a Lawful altar.  I was hoping for
Neutral but I at least wanted cross-aligned.

Then I ventured back into the main dungeon looking for a neutral
altar.  I searched every room fearful of finding another artifact.  I
was planning on wishing for the PYEC but I had already found a bones
pile with Excalibur.  If I had found one more artifact I might not
have gotten my artifact wish and without the option of a scroll of
charging, losing wishes is bad news.  Good news, I found a neutral
altar on level 11.  So I fountain dipped and left the PYEC at the
Oracle.

Doing the Quest without MR was kind of scary because of the
chameleons.  But I think I managed to hunt all of them down without
having to fight an arch-lich so that was a relief.   Fortunately I had
price-ID'ed a ring of polymorph control and with three tries I managed
to turn into a xorn.  Fighting the Master Assassin was the closest
that I came to dying; he hit harder than I expected.  Next time I'll
prepare better and leave my potions in the main inventory.  I have
grown accustomed to leaving useful scrolls and potions safely stowed
away in bags that I forget to take them out prior to battles.

After completing the quest I went back to Minetown, converted the
altar and waited "n100s" until an orc showed up.  I wanted to do a
same race-sacrifice while I was still chaotic so that I could summon
Yeenoghu in case I needed an extra demon prince to help me acquire the
Amulet.  Unfortunately my pet white dragon (who served me well) killed
Juiblex.  I also wanted to wield Grimtooth since it's conveniently an
orcish dagger.  Finally by turn 55,000 (!) it was time to self-convert
to Neutral so that I could pick up the PYEC.  To pass the time while I
waited for an orc to show up and enough sacrifice fodder I would
teleport in and out of the general store in minetown.  The Kops were a
push-over and I collected a big stack of cream pies.  The quest was
also a good place to acquire teleportitis once I had my ring of
teleport control.  I start the game by price ID'ing rings and I
generally drop a ring from any pair or low price onto sinks.
Typically I'm tripping over rings by the end of the game so I don't
worry about losing a lot.  For fun I charged a ring of protection up
to +4 with the PYEC and wore that a lot and then when I found another
ring of protection I would try to bring it to +5 though I was
unsuccessful.

The game would have taken longer still if I hadn't found a black
unicorn on level 9.  For the alignment conversion I killed the black
unicorn, threw it over my shoulder, zapped a wand of digging twice,
and then teleported to the altar.

>From then on the game was pretty simple.  I b/u/c tested all of the
orcish armor, daggers and short swords that I found and ended up with
a +4 orcish helm and a blessed orcish short sword but nothing else
enchanted.  I found out at the end of the game that my found short
sword was +1; I had decided to wield it instead of my starting weapon
because I figured that since it was blessed it was at least +0 and
therefore at least equal to my starting weapon (*pats self on back*)
Grimtooth was rustproofed but I'm not sure if that's because of a
prayer or if it started that way.

I had expected rust monsters and disenchanters to be a challenge but
in the early game I let pets fight them and in the endgame I either
teleported past them or else took off my +4 helmet and only hit them
with Grimtooth.  Most other monsters were easily dealt with either by
melee or thrown orcish daggers.  I personally believe that weapon
enchantment is overrated.  Sure it's nice to kill things quickly but
even +0 weapons did just fine for me.  Basically, it just took
longer.

I did some minor polypiling in order to acquire some scare monster
scrolls, get some better potions, complete my ring set, and get an
amulet of reflection.  An I also threw in 65 or so blessed cream
pies.  I managed to turn 25 blessed blank scrolls into 3 scrolls of
scare monster before my wand ran out of charges.  Interestingly
enough, I never used any scrolls of scare monster or scary-sounding
instruments.  By the time I had a scroll of scare monster or was able
to wish for an instrument I didn't need either anymore.  Getting the
amulet of reflection from polying saved me a wish.  The real goal from
the polypiling was, of course, wands of teleport and I ended up with a
pretty good supply of them.

Speaking of wishes, I only used 3 because the RNG gave me most of
everything I needed.
1: blessed Platinum Yendorian Express Card ~ for MR and charging ~
from a fountain and picked it up 35,000 turns later;
2: blessed fixed +3 orcish chain mail ~ from Vlad's throne ~ at this
point I didn't really need anything but I figured that this would be a
nice touch;
3: a blessed crystal ball ~ wished for this right before going to the
planes.  It turned out to be quite handy as I would let vortices
engulf me and then find the portals.
~ I never rubbed my magic lamp.  Personally I hate having my light
sources go out so I was happy to have a magic lamp throughout the
game.
The castle wand was 0:1, apparently, so I would have needed the PYEC
to charge it.

Here's a summary of end-game turns:
71840 - disturb wizard;
72815 - enter Plane of Earth (thanks to jumping, self-teleport, plenty
of food, and a benign mysterious force;
72886 - enter Plane of Air
73008 - enter Plane of Water
73053 - enter Astral Plane
73219 - offer Amulet



The planes were pretty easy and I levitated and wand of
teleportation'ed my way through them.  On the plane of water I would
wait for several air bubbles to come together and then zap a wand of
secret door detection.  It worked on the second try.  "Funny thing
happened to me on my way to the Astral Plane ..."  My typical
procedure is to zoom my way through each plane and then stand next to
the portal and reset my gear.  On the Plane of Water I dropped a bunch
of stuff like wands of cold, digging, etc but I didn't want to drop my
crystal ball for fear of breaking it.  (I suppose I'm superstitious)
Instead I threw it, and sure enough I floated right into the portal
and onto Astral with my ring of conflict still on.  Ooops!  Well, it
turns out that 2 of the 4 hostile angels were carrying wands of
death!

With those wands, my teleport wands, conflict, and jumping I strolled
about on a 3-altar tour and Voila!  Party-Time!

I'm sure I left something out but as the days go by more and more of
the game is fading from my memory so here's the dump.
